% ============================================================================
% phase-change-alt-name — for the phase changes where the SAME source names
% an alternate word, the alternate name (adj-facts-stdlib, PHYSICS).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `states-of-matter.adj`
% (`phase_change_name(change, name)`, ONE primary name per phase-change
% direction: melting, freezing, vaporization, condensation, sublimation,
% deposition): a new `phase_change_alt_name(change, alt_name)` table names
% the OLDER or ALTERNATE word the SAME already-cited LibreTexts pages state
% for a direction, decoded from spans already sitting unused inside
% `states-of-matter.adj`'s own header and provenance block — no new
% WebFetch. That table's `name` column has room for only the PRIMARY name;
% the SAME already-quoted sentences also carry a second word for three of
% the six directions that never made it into a row:
%
%   solid_to_liquid → fusion
%     "an older term that you may see sometimes is fusion"
%   liquid_to_solid → solidification
%     "some textbooks also use the word solidification for this process"
%   liquid_to_gas → boiling
%     "which is more often referred to as \"boiling\""
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? phase_change_alt_name(solid_to_liquid, $AltName)      % fusion
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table like
% `states-of-matter.adj`'s own `phase_change_name`): each `row` lowers to a
% ground relation `phase_change_alt_name(change, alt_name)` carrying the
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the
% engine returns the alternate name WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero
% model calls, and abstains on any direction the cited spans do not give an
% alternate name for (`gas_to_liquid`/`solid_to_gas`/`gas_to_solid`, whose
% own already-cited spans in `states-of-matter.adj` state only the single
% primary name — condensation, sublimation, deposition — with no second
% word).
%
% Only THREE of `states-of-matter.adj`'s six phase-change directions have
% an alternate name in the ALREADY-cited spans; this table is deliberately
% narrow rather than inventing a synonym the source does not state. The
% query also runs BACKWARD — bind an alternate name and recall which
% direction it names:
%
%     ? phase_change_alt_name($Change, boiling)   % liquid_to_gas
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME solid_to_liquid span
% already quoted inside `states-of-matter.adj`'s own provenance block, from
% the SAME LibreTexts "Melting, Freezing, and Sublimation" page that table
% already cites — no new WebFetch, only a different schema decoding a part
% of each already-verified quote. An ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance
% envelope (per `states-of-matter.adj`'s own convention), so
% `source`/`locator`/`trust` below hold the solid_to_liquid span; the
% liquid_to_solid/liquid_to_gas rows' own spans are listed above for
% independent auditability. `trust consensus` — the same tier
% `states-of-matter.adj` already carries (LibreTexts is a secondary
% teaching reference, not a primary source).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table phase_change_alt_name {
    columns change, alt_name

    row (solid_to_liquid, fusion)
    row (liquid_to_solid, solidification)
    row (liquid_to_gas, boiling)

    source "The process of a solid becoming a liquid is called melting (an older term that you may see sometimes is fusion)."
    locator "https://chem.libretexts.org/Courses/Madera_Community_College/MacArthur_Chemistry_3A_v_1.2/09:_Attractive_Forces/9.03:_Phase_Transitions/9.3.01:_Melting_Freezing_and_Sublimation"
    trust consensus
}
